{
  "as_of": "2026-09-27",
  "scope": "HTTP response Content-Disposition syntax and conservative filename admission in Go and Python. Parsed and raw final/informational blocks; Python MCP delivery. Simulated peers and resolvers, not a deployment or real filesystem-write test.",
  "changes": [
    "Treat HTTP Content-Disposition as a singleton field and reject duplicate case-insensitive parameter names before generic MIME recovery can merge them.",
    "Parse token/quoted parameter grammar with its permitted spaces around equals; retain unknown disposition types and ordinary extension parameters, with at most 128 parameters.",
    "Validate unquoted extended values, percent triplets, UTF-8 or legacy ISO-8859-1 decoding, and optional well-formed RFC 5646 language tags including all fixed grandfathered forms.",
    "Check both filename and filename* independently while preserving the valid fallback pair. Reject selected path, control/format, noncharacter, whitespace, quoted-pair and residual-percent ambiguities without rewriting the header.",
    "Keep MIME body-part Content-Disposition metadata separate because RFC 6266 explicitly excludes payload fields. No new Snort SID."
  ],
